  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Crafting A Simulation Design**\n\nI\'m now carefully analyzing the user\'s request, focusing on designing an authoritative and deeply accurate interactive Vietnamese electromagnetic wave refraction simulation. The goal is a workbench that provides characteristic identification capabilities, and I\'m prioritizing accuracy and interactivity in my approach.\n\n\n**Designing The Architecture**\n\nI\'m now conceptualizing the application\'s architecture as **EMRefract Lab**, or *Ph\303\262ng th\303\255 nghi\341\273\207m M\303\264 ph\341\273\217ng & Nh\341\272\255n d\341\272\241ng Kh\303\272c x\341\272\241 S\303\263ng \304\220i\341\273\207n t\341\273\253*. My focus is on creating a user-friendly Vietnamese interface. I\'m aiming for a clinical optics/RF lab aesthetic with a crisp, light-mode design. Key elements like a `#F8FAFC` canvas, `#F1F5F9` surfaces, and `#0F172A` typography are being considered for this refined layout, keeping with calibrated azure color palettes.\n\n\n**Defining Color Palettes & Fonts**\n\nI\'m solidifying the aesthetic foundation. The color scheme will use cobalt for primary accents, emerald for transmitted waves, amber for Brewster angles, and rose for reflected/TIR scenarios. I\'m choosing `Plus Jakarta Sans` for the UI/display and `IBM Plex Mono` for data and equations. Loading these from Google Fonts is the next step.\n\n\n**Visualizing EM Wave Phenomena**\n\nI am now focusing on the interactive visualization elements. This encompasses ray trajectories, normal lines, angle arcs, and E/H field polarization vectors. I\'m keen to implement Total Internal Reflection (TIR) with an evanescent wave display, showing the phase shift. I want direct canvas manipulation for user interaction, allowing them to drag the wave source and probe fields. Layer toggles will offer various display modes for wavefronts and rays.\n\n\n**Implementing Interactive Controls**\n\nI\'m now focusing on user interaction and control design. I\'m implementing a suite of playback controls \342\200\223 Play/Pause, Step, Reset, and Wave Speed \342\200\223 for real-time wave behavior adjustment. The UI will feature detailed controls, including presets for real materials with refractive indices, and sliders for angle precision, including key angles like Brewster\'s and critical angles.\n\n\n**Defining Interactive Controls**\n\nI\'m now focusing on adding interactive controls for the simulation. I\'ll include options for flipping media and adjusting the electromagnetic source. I\'m prioritizing user control of frequency, polarization (TE, TM, and unpolarized), and guided scenarios such as common refraction, Brewster\'s angle, total internal reflection, and high-index fiber optics. Specific frequency bands, like visible light and RF, are in scope, too.\n\n\n**Classifying Propagation Regimes**\n\nI\'m now implementing the real-time regime and characteristic classifier, focusing on automatic identification and explanation of the active propagation regimes. I\'m prioritizing clear and concise text with associated icons for: Normal Incidence, Refraction (increasing/decreasing index), Brewster\'s Angle, Critical Angle, TIR with Evanescent Wave, and Homogenous Media. Precise quantitative metrics are computed and displayed for user reference.\n\n\n**Calculating Fresnel Parameters**\n\nI am now focusing on the real-time calculations and display of key Fresnel parameters. I\'m prioritizing the display of the transmitted angle, deviation, reflectance coefficients (Rs, Rp, Reff) and transmittance coefficients (Ts, Tp, Teff). I\'m implementing phase shift calculations for both reflection and transmission, including the 180-degree phase flip in denser media and the Goos-H\303\244nchen shift in TIR. Finally, I will calculate wave velocities, wavelengths, wave numbers, impedances, and wavelength compression ratio. I want all of these calculated automatically and presented for the user.\n\n\n**Designing Inverse Identification**\n\nI am now adding the \"Inverse Identification Laboratory.\" Users can input observed lab data or sample from the current simulation. This mode supports measured incident/refracted angles or critical angle observation. The goal is to identify the characteristics of the propagating regime. This design specifically targets the user\'s request for identifying wave propagation characteristics from data.\n\n\n**Developing Identification Capabilities**\n\nI\'m now integrating the inverse identification engine, allowing users to input measured data like reflectance and wavelength ratios. The system will calculate the unknown refractive index and related properties. Importantly, it will auto-match these derived properties to known materials, complete with confidence scores and a check for inconsistencies, such as those due to absorptive media.\n\n\n**Integrating Verification Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing a \"N\341\272\241p v\303\240o m\303\264 ph\341\273\217ng\" button to visually verify identified parameters directly in the live canvas, linking identification directly to simulation. Further, I\'m designing a multi-trial comparison log with an interactive table to record simulation states for comparison.\n\n\n**Defining UI/UX Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on UI components, prioritizing a two-zone asymmetric split console with a fixed-width left control deck and a flexible main stage. I want a wave canvas at the top, followed by an automatic propagation characteristic identifier. Key elements include the preset scenarios selector, material selectors, angle sliders, and a polarization segmented control. I\'m also considering the \"Ghi nh\341\272\255n ph\303\251p \304\221o\" button, for measuring the current wave state.\n\n\n\n**Designing Inverse Identification View**\n\nNow I\'m designing the Inverse Identification View. It\'ll feature a side-by-side design. On the left: a measurement input form with Vietnamese validation messages. On the right: identification results including medium parameters, propagating regime diagnostics, material match ranking, and a \"Load into Simulation\" button. I\'m also planning a Lab Measurement Log View as a full tabular dataset with load, delete, clear, and CSV download options.\n\n\n**Validating Calculations & Identification**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing comprehensive verification tests. I\'m focusing on rigorous unit tests, specifically in `emPhysics.test.ts` and `identifier.test.ts`. I\'m going through exhaustive checks on Snell\'s Law and Total Internal Reflection, especially verifying the behavior of evanescent waves. Brewster\'s angle is also being carefully scrutinized to confirm near-zero reflection and maximum transmission.\n\n\n**Verifying User Journeys**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into user journey verification using the Playwright CLI Browser. I\'m focusing on two key scenarios. The first, focusing on live wave refraction and automatic characteristic identification, is already complete. The second, targeting inverse wave characteristic and material identification, is next.\n\n\n**Validating Interactive Journeys**\n\nI\'ve just validated both the core wave simulation and the inverse identification workbenches on the target desktop display. I can confirm seamless switching between views and the successful rendering of the live wavefront animation on the canvas. I\'ve also validated key user journeys, confirming robust error handling and proper data persistence with a fully functional measurement log.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "hcgGa7ylKrmA3LUPtMLe4A4"
