  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing Visual Tools**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focusing on designing visual toolbars. The follow-up request really hit the nail on the head. Adding visual instruments to make it more intuitive seems to be the direction we\'re headed.\n\n\n**Developing Visual Instrument Bar**\n\nI\'m now implementing the visual instrument bar, specifically the protractor overlay, directly onto the `WaveCanvas`. The aim is a crisp, intuitive optical protractor, with degree markings and tick marks, that users can toggle on the canvas. I\'m focusing on the $0^\\circ\\text{\342\200\223}90^\\circ$ calibration and its semi-transparent look.\n\n\n**Visualizing Energy Flux**\n\nI\'m now designing an animated Energy Flux & Wave Comparison Strip for direct visual feedback. This will include an animated dual-segment bar showing the split between reflection and transmission, and a visual wavelength compression gauge comparing wavelengths. I\'m also adding zoom controls directly on the canvas toolbar to inspect boundary interfaces.\n\n\n**Implementing Angle Sweep Mode**\n\nI\'m now incorporating an \"Angle Sweep Mode\" button for interactive exploration. This will smoothly sweep the incident angle from 0 to 85 degrees and back, allowing users to observe the beam\'s behavior through normal incidence, the Brewster angle, critical angle, and total internal reflection, all while watching the energy bar, protractor, and Fresnel chart update. I\'m focusing on clean Lucide icons and responsive handlers for every control.\n\n\n"
