  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Architecture**\n\nI\'m incredibly pleased with how smoothly the architectural design is progressing. My focus right now is ensuring that every single user requirement and skill rule is flawlessly integrated into the architecture on the first pass, before any code is even considered.\n\n\n**Refining the Backend**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously refining the backend architecture, specifically focusing on the integration of the database layer and authentication. I\'m ensuring strict adherence to ESM rules within the `src/db/*` directory, using TypeScript for all relative database imports. Key database schemas have been defined, including the `users` and `measurements` tables, with crucial fields and relationships meticulously specified. I am making it all work seamlessly and securely with Firebase Auth and Cloud SQL (PostgreSQL).\n\n\n**Defining the Database Schema**\n\nI\'m now deep in the database schema definition. I\'ve laid out the `measurements` table with all necessary fields like `incidentAngleDeg`, `refractedAngleDeg`, and so on. Also, the `searchLogs` table is shaping up nicely, including fields for user ID, query, summary, and supporting information. I\'ve got the drizzle `relations` sorted out for seamless interaction. The database connection setup is using a connection pool for efficiency and proper configuration options.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve just added robust error handling to the database repository. I\'m especially happy with the two-layer system that wraps raw database errors into sanitized ones. I\'m also finding `onConflictDoUpdate` is really useful with the `getOrCreateUser` function. The CRUD functions for measurements and search logs are also now implemented, and I\'ve ensured that each is strictly scoped by user ID.\n\n\n**Verifying User Authentication**\n\nI\'m now implementing robust authentication, verifying tokens using `adminAuth.verifyIdToken(token)` and syncing users via `getOrCreateUser`. It\'s imperative that the UI remains isolated from backend details. Signed-in users should have seamless persistence and synchronization, while guests retain full simulator access. We must also ensure the top bar strictly adheres to the three-zone structure.\n\n\n**Refining UI Zone Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the UI, specifically the top bar\'s design. The 3-zone rule is paramount, ensuring Zone 3 has at most one primary call-to-action, plus any necessary Sign-In/Account controls in a compact and clean fashion. I\'m considering integrating the Google Sign-In button inside the `Ph\303\251p \304\221o` bar to maximize simplicity and maintain zero pills.\n\n\n\n**Extracting Data & Parsing**\n\nI\'m now focusing on parsing responses, especially from Markdown. I\'m keen to avoid forced schemas and extract data naturally. My goal is to extract `answer`, `sources`, `extractedN`, and `searchQueries` while strictly filtering `web.uri` to `http:`/`https:` to avoid security issues. I\'ll also add a \"Ph\303\242n t\303\255ch b\341\273\221i c\341\272\243nh hi\341\273\207n t\341\272\241i\" button to provide quick queries and a button to apply a refractive index to the simulation.\n\n\n**Validating API Endpoints**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `POST /api/search/material` endpoint. I\'ve confirmed that this endpoint works for all users. Furthermore, I\'ve secured it such that if an `Authorization: Bearer` token is present and valid, the query history will be stored in PostgreSQL\'s `searchLogs` table for the corresponding signed-in user. Security is paramount, so the endpoint only interacts with the database when authenticated. I\'m validating and bounding the input length (`<= 300 chars`). It uses `GEMINI_API_KEY` exclusively on the server, and propagates upstream errors without fabricated fallback responses.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Apparatus**\n\nI\'m now deep into the visual design, currently focusing on the breadboard base. I\'m imagining a beveled slate surface with brushed aluminum texture and M6 threaded hole grid. I\'m also planning stainless-steel vibration-isolation legs and a raised goniometer ring.\n\n\n**Designing the Physics Apparatus**\n\nI\'m now deep in the design of the physics apparatus. I\'m focusing on polished chrome aperture barrels, beveled optical glass blocks, and a receiver arm. The key is to dynamically update materials based on physical parameters like refractive index and wavelength, to provide an accurate simulation.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Wavefronts**\n\nI\'m now engrossed in visualizing the wave behavior during total internal reflection (TIR). I am visualizing both 3D electromagnetic waves and beam visualizations. The key is to pre-allocate vertex buffers and use `THREE.BufferGeometry` and `THREE.InstancedMesh` with a scratch object `_matrix` to minimize overhead.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Light**\n\nI\'m now implementing the light path visualization. I\'m focusing on true relative phase velocities for the incident, reflected, and refracted waves. I\'m using THREE.js with PBR lighting and WebGL guard, using ACESFilmicToneMapping and a warm directional key light. I\'ve set up a horizon-matched light studio background and THREE.Fog for a seamless look.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Control Dock**\n\nI\'m now implementing the ergonomic, collapsible bottom control dock. My goal is to maximize the viewport while offering concise controls. I\'m focusing on a minimal top-left spec readout and a single, compact bottom dock. The dock will include sliders for key parameters and quick-access scenario buttons.\n\n\n**Orchestrating the Development**\n\nI am now structuring the development into sequential waves. Wave 1 focuses on the database and backend with defined file creation and updates. I\'ll then move into Wave 2, which involves pushing and verifying the Cloud SQL schema via rpc actions. Finally, Wave 3 will integrate the frontend, encompassing authentication contexts, the 3D lab components, and search and measurement integration.\n\n\n"
