  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering Crucial Findings**\n\nI\'m incredibly excited! I just reviewed `r6-3d-lab.png` and `r6-3d-motion.webm`. The 3D optical bench looks like a genuine precision instrument. Our visual inspection immediately identified **two** critical defects. We\'re on the right track; the visual inspection is crucial, and it\'s producing immediate results.\n\n\n**Analyzing Prism Positioning**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the positioning of the dielectric prism. Specifically, the relationship between the dielectric glass block and its cradle is central. The image and video confirm that the incident and reflected beams exist in the `-Z` region (Medium 1), while the refracted beam is in the `+Z` region (Medium 2). Also, the cradle IS located on the `+Z` side. The rotation seems consistent with Three.js\' coordinate system.\n\n\n**Investigating Optical Alignment**\n\nI\'m now deeply focused on the apparent misalignment within the setup. The critical revelation comes from frame `00:04`. I am perplexed by the placement of the laser emitter and photodetector relative to the prism and cradle. The initial assumption that the cradle and prism are in the same plane may be wrong. The emerald refracted beam appears to travel *into* the glass block, but I need to re-evaluate based on the coordinate system to confirm that impression. This needs more attention.\n\n\n**Confirming Coordinate System**\n\nI\'m now completely clear on the coordinate system; it all makes sense now!  The camera\'s perspective has been internalized: screen-left is Medium 1 and -X, and screen-front-right is +Z (Medium 2).  I\'ve re-examined the extruded geometry and rotation, and am satisfied that the transformations produce the correct orientation. I\'m now focusing on the shifted wireframe edge in the image and the position of the laser emitter and photodetector from the camera\'s point of view.\n\n\n**Orienting the Semicircle**\n\nI\'m working through the semicircle\'s orientation relative to the glass block and coordinate system.  It seems the dark semicircle\'s flat edge lies along z=0. The glass block\'s arc appears to go into the -Z half-plane, which may mean the dark semicircle is oriented into -Z. Re-examining frame `00:04`, I see the laser emitter is roughly at 9 o\'clock (-X) and the photodetector around 12 o\'clock (-Z). I\'m still puzzled about the dark semicircle\'s rotation.\n\n\n**Clarifying Prism Orientation**\n\nI\'ve just had a major breakthrough regarding the prism\'s orientation! I completely understand the coordinate system now: the camera is positioned to view the setup, and this gives us a clear perspective. Examining frame `00:04`, I see the incident and reflected beams on the upper-left (`-Z`, Medium 1), while the dark semicircle is on the lower-right (`+Z`, Medium 2). I\'m looking closely at `r6-3d-lab.png` and can see the emerald beam goes into the glass!\n\n\n**Resolving Geometrical Discrepancies**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical inconsistency, specifically the apparent positional inversion between the cyan wireframe and the dark cradle. Both the prism and the cradle utilize an identical `dShape` and `rotateX(Math.PI/2)`, which is confounding. However, by carefully analyzing the positions of the brass posts and the effect of the `ExtrudeGeometry` combined with the rotation, I\'m beginning to unravel this apparent paradox, but I am not certain. I believe the block height is the crucial factor.\n\n\n**Confirming Vertical Alignment**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial observation. The prism\'s top face is 1.24 units above the cradle, creating an offset that the images reflect perfectly. The vertical blue lines confirm that the 3D geometry is aligned, solving that issue. I then focused on the emitter angle, which rotates around +Y by +45\302\260. This aligns with the expected coordinate transformation from (0, 0, -3.05) to (-X, -Z). Also, the photodetector rotation behaves as predicted.\n\n\n"
